javadrills/14-jdbc-data-access/03-batch-.explanation:
Flush when the chunk fills, and once more after the loop if anything is left:

```java
try (var ps = c.prepareStatement(INSERT_SQL)) {
    int pending = 0;
    for (var r : rows) {
        ps.setString(1, r.sku());
        ps.setInt(2, r.qty());
        ps.addBatch();
        if (++pending == chunk) { inserted += sum(ps.executeBatch()); pending = 0; }
    }
    if (pending > 0) inserted += sum(ps.executeBatch());
}
c.commit();
```

The tests read the batches the fake received. Dropping the final `if (pending > 0)` flush loses the last row of five (the 2-2-1 test sees 4). Flushing after the loop unconditionally sends an empty batch when the rows divide evenly (the 4-row test expects exactly two batches). Committing after every chunk instead of once means a failure in chunk 3 leaves chunks 1 and 2 in the table (the one-transaction test counts exactly one commit). Checking `rows.isEmpty()` after opening the transaction fails the boundary test, which expects an untouched connection.

