Index the orders by id, then fill them from one query per chunk of ids:

```java
var linesById = new LinkedHashMap<Long, List<Line>>();
for (long id : ids) linesById.put(id, new ArrayList<>());
for (int from = 0; from < ids.size(); from += maxIds) {
    var chunk = ids.subList(from, Math.min(ids.size(), from + maxIds));
    String sql = LINES_PREFIX + String.join(", ", Collections.nCopies(chunk.size(), "?")) + LINES_SUFFIX;
    try (var ps = c.prepareStatement(sql)) {
        for (int i = 0; i < chunk.size(); i++) ps.setLong(i + 1, chunk.get(i));
        try (var rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
            while (rs.next()) linesById.get(rs.getLong("order_id")).add(new Line(rs.getString("sku"), rs.getInt("qty")));
        }
    }
}
```

The tests count the queries in the fake's log. One lines query per order, the N+1 shape, gives six queries for five orders (the budget test wants two). Ignoring `maxIds` sends all five ids in one statement (the chunk test wants 1 + 3 queries). Running the lines query for a customer with no orders sends `IN ()`, which is invalid SQL (the boundary test wants one query only). Grouping into a `HashMap` keyed by order without pre-seeding every id drops orders that have no lines.

