javadrills/14-jdbc-data-access/05-sql-nu.explanation:
Read the primitive, then ask whether it was NULL:

```java
static Customer map(ResultSet rs) throws SQLException {
    long id = rs.getLong("id");
    String name = rs.getString("name");
    int pct = rs.getInt("discount_pct");
    Integer discount = rs.wasNull() ? null : pct;
    BigDecimal credit = rs.getBigDecimal("credit_limit");
    return new Customer(id, name, discount, credit);
}
```

`wasNull` reports on the LAST getter called, so it must come straight after `getInt`. The tests catch the plausible wrong mappers. Plain `rs.getInt("discount_pct")` turns NULL into 0 (the null-discount test). "Fixing" it with `pct == 0 ? null : pct` erases real zero discounts (the zero test). Calling `wasNull()` after `getBigDecimal` instead of after `getInt` asks about the wrong column, so a row with a credit limit and no discount comes out as 0 (the mixed-row test).

